1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:08,700
Imagine your entire ministry is, uh, building a new headquarters, but, you know, every single department is using a totally different set of blueprints.

2
00:00:08,860 --> 00:00:14,320
Right. The plumbers have last month's plans, and the electricians are using a version from, like, twenty twenty-four.

3
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:18,580
Exactly. And the architects are, I don't know, sketching on napkins.

4
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:19,590
You would never break ground.

5
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:25,500
So today we're taking a deep dive into how governments actually map their digital systems.

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:28,780
We are looking at enterprise architecture repositories.

7
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:30,190
Or EA repositories, yeah.

8
00:00:30,210 --> 00:00:39,750
Right, EA repositories. Our mission here is to uncover why establishing a single source of truth is basically the ultimate structural fix for

9
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:41,800
fragmented, duplicated systems.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,180
Yeah, because a repository is, um, it's the single agreed place where your architecture actually lives.

11
00:00:47,280 --> 00:00:49,710
So it's about having one accurate picture of the organization.

12
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:55,680
Exactly. Instead of having dozens of private conflicting copies floating around, it's the foundational step.

13
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:59,980
You really have to set this up first because everything else you do just governs what goes into it.

14
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:06,170
But wait, if we're dealing with massive government frameworks, restricting everything to one location seems kind of risky.

15
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:11,010
I mean, shouldn't different ministries maintain their own backups of these architecture diagrams

16
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:12,440
just in case?

17
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:16,640
See, that intuition is exactly where organizations start to drift into trouble.

18
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:18,700
Really? How so?

19
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:22,040
Well, a second copy doesn't actually function as a backup.

20
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,820
It completely undermines the single source of truth.

21
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:28,920
Oh, I see. Because the moment a ministry saves their own version-

22
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:34,700
The architectures drift. Exactly. Soon enough, you have different teams working from totally conflicting pictures.

23
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:40,660
And instead of discussing the actual architecture, teams just end up arguing over whose document is authoritative.

24
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:45,570
Right. Because if it lives in an isolated slide deck somewhere, it is outdated almost immediately.

25
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:49,720
You either have one authoritative place, or you effectively have none.

26
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:58,380
Okay, so if the main danger is drift, then whatever lives inside this single location has to be completely authoritative and comprehensive.

27
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:03,200
It does. But are we talking about, like, a massive IT inventory list,

28
00:02:03,860 --> 00:02:11,160
or is it more like a city planner's master ledger that tracks both the physical infrastructure and the zoning laws?

29
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:16,540
I actually love that analogy. It's very much like a master ledger, and it needs to hold three specific components.

30
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:17,780
Okay, what's the first one?

31
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:22,530
First, you have the structural layers. This is where you connect the dots between what the business does,

32
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:26,380
uh, the capabilities, and the tech and data that actually make it happen.

33
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:31,430
So mapping capabilities, data domains, applications, and shared technology.

34
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:34,220
Exactly. Then the second component is mapping the relationships.

35
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:37,579
You have to show how those layers connect using shared entities.

36
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:39,650
Right. Okay. And the third component?

37
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:41,460
The third is the decision log.

38
00:02:41,580 --> 00:02:48,920
Ah, yes. Looking at the case studies in our notes, this logs what the enterprise architecture board ruled, and I guess

39
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:53,720
why they ruled that way. Yes, exactly. But it's easy to just say, you know, document the decisions.

40
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:58,160
How does that actually stop duplicated work across an entire government?

41
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,070
Well, look at the Progressa education sector case study from our research.

42
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:02,910
Right, the education one.

43
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:08,860
Yeah. So their repository publicly maps data domains to explicit owners.

44
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:12,620
For example, the person domain is owned by the identity authority.

45
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:15,549
Okay. And the learner domain is owned by the learner registry.

46
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:19,490
Meanwhile, examination results belong to the examination authority.

47
00:03:19,780 --> 00:03:22,410
Oh, wow. So the mechanism here is really enforcement.

48
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,200
Exactly. It's all about enforcement.

49
00:03:24,260 --> 00:03:32,500
So if the examination authority decides, "Hey, we want to build our own student database to track test scores," the IT architects can just check the repository.

50
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:38,720
Spot on. They see the learner registry already owns that domain, and they can just deny the budget for the duplicated work.

51
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:40,100
That makes a lot of sense.

52
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:46,440
The repository logs the board's ruling that the examination authority has to consume data from the learner registry.

53
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:48,040
They can't just re-key it all.

54
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:57,060
And because that decision and the reasoning behind it is visible to everyone, it prevents those siloed, costly to change systems from getting funded in the first place.

55
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:00,240
Exactly. It stops the duplication before it even starts.

56
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:09,040
But enforcing those interconnected layers and data owners sounds like it would require this massive multi-million dollar software platform.

57
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:16,500
I mean, does an organization need to buy a huge enterprise IT system just to set this ledger up?

58
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:17,700
Actually, no,

59
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:20,310
and that's a really common misconception.

60
00:04:20,899 --> 00:04:23,980
A repository is a strict discipline, not a software product.

61
00:04:24,120 --> 00:04:27,440
Well, wait, really? You don't need a massive investment to start.

62
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:35,140
Not at all. A well-structured spreadsheet or even a shared wiki works perfectly as long as it holds those layers, relationships, and decisions.

63
00:04:35,460 --> 00:04:39,500
So the critical factor is just that everyone uses it as the one single source.

64
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:43,380
Exactly. The structure and the discipline matter way more than the tool itself.

65
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:49,080
Many countries run a highly successful early enterprise architecture entirely on a spreadsheet.

66
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:51,310
That is wild to think about, just a spreadsheet.

67
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:56,500
Right. You only graduate to a dedicated tool when scaling makes a spreadsheet unmanageable.

68
00:04:56,540 --> 00:05:01,160
Got it. The repository's real function is acting as the object both sides can point at.

69
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:07,600
When a minister briefs the cabinet and an IT architect is designing a system, they are looking at the exact same picture.

70
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:11,310
It creates a shared language between the business side and the IT side.

71
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:19,980
Precisely. So before worrying about complex governance frameworks or super expensive tools, organizations just have to establish that single store.

72
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:22,160
Because if private copies multiply,

73
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:25,120
no governance can save the architecture.

74
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:27,860
Yeah. It all comes down to eliminating that second copy.

75
00:05:27,880 --> 00:05:30,190
Well, as you head back to your own work today, consider this:

76
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:38,060
If an entire government's digital architecture can be unified by a well-structured spreadsheet simply acting as the undisputed source of truth,

